# The mbedTLS release this tree builds TLS termination against (#125).
#
# Same doctrine as libuv.pin: the REPO declares the version, so adopting a new
# one is an ordinary commit that propagates through git. A native dependency
# each machine resolves independently is the "works on mine, not on yours"
# hazard, and for a C ABI mismatch it does not fail politely.
#
# We do NOT depend on a distro or system OpenSSL. `aion/tls` is an OPT-IN
# aux system: nothing that merely uses aion or hyperion gets a crypto library on its
# load path. That is not a new decision -- mnemosyne.asd and aion.asd both
# already refuse cl+ssl in those words, both citing ADR-0011's failure, where
# Woo's load-time libev made every desktop bundle die on a clean machine.
#
# Consequence worth stating: a desktop bundle that does not serve HTTPS carries
# nothing at all, and under ADR-0008 desktop is plaintext-on-localhost anyway.
# The cost lands only on a bundle that actually terminates TLS.

# --- why 4.1 and not 3.6 LTS -----------------------------------------------
#
# Counter-intuitive, and measured rather than assumed (#125):
#
#   3.6 LTS  released March 2024, three-year lifetime -> ends MARCH 2027
#   4.1      the first 4.x LTS, released March 2026   -> ends ~MARCH 2029
#
# Both from BRANCHES.md in the respective tarballs. Choosing 3.6 in late 2026
# does not avoid the 4.x migration, it SCHEDULES it inside eighteen months and
# against a support deadline -- after doing the transcription once already.
#
# On size: 4.1.1 compiles 110 sources where 3.6.2 compiles 108 -- the same, not
# the fivefold increase the 437-file tree suggests, and not the reduction an
# earlier count of 89 claimed. Both of those numbers were mine and both were
# wrong: 437 counts tests, programs, examples and the vendored framework, and 89
# came from finding two file(GLOB) calls and missing the add_subdirectory lines
# that pull in extras, platform and utilities. The list is settled by LINKING,
# not by reading -- see scripts/mbedtls-sources.lisp.

# --- why BearSSL is not here -----------------------------------------------
#
# It was the other candidate and is MIT, with a better I/O model for a socket
# libuv owns (a buf/ack engine that never touches an fd). It is out on
# capability: BR_TLS10/11/12 and nothing beyond -- NO TLS 1.3 -- and every
# source file in 0.6 is dated August 2018, still the latest release. For an
# internet-facing on-prem server that is disqualifying before the dormancy
# argument is needed. Recorded so it is not re-proposed for its elegance.

version 4.1.1
sha256  3359a349e23db3d5536fcee032ae7b2ecbfc08972fab643089b5cbf2a375c98c
url     https://github.com/Mbed-TLS/mbedtls/releases/download/mbedtls-4.1.1/mbedtls-4.1.1.tar.bz2

# Licence: dual Apache-2.0 OR GPL-2.0-or-later; WE TAKE APACHE-2.0. Permissive
# and fine to ship beside MIT code, but it is not MIT -- it carries a patent
# grant and NOTICE obligations, so the licence text ships with any artifact
# that carries the library. Approved by the maintainer 2026-09-15.
licence Apache-2.0

# --- staying current -------------------------------------------------------
#
# This is the field that matters most on this pin and least on libuv's. A TLS
# implementation publishes security releases several times a year; upgrading is
# easy and NOTICING is the hard part. `reviewed` is the only thing separating
# "no advisory affects us" from "nobody has looked" -- identical from outside,
# and only one of them is fine. Bump it when you look, including when the answer
# is "nothing to do". See docs/versioning-and-pinning.md.

advisories https://github.com/Mbed-TLS/mbedtls/security/advisories
reviewed   2026-09-15

# --- the source set --------------------------------------------------------
#
# mbedTLS 4.x does NOT name its sources. The crypto is a second project inside
# the tarball whose CMake says `file(GLOB src_crypto "*.c")`, so there is no
# upstream list to diff ours against -- which is the check libuv.pin's
# "verified against 1.52.1" relies on and this pin cannot have.
#
# So the property is different: DID THE SET CHANGE BETWEEN PINS. mbedtls.sources
# is the committed answer for 4.1.1, and scripts/mbedtls-sources.lisp re-globs a
# tree and reports what appeared or vanished BY NAME. The digest below ties this
# pin to that manifest so a version bump cannot quietly keep an old one.
#
# Not bureaucracy. Verified against the real 4.1.1 -> 4.2.0 step: 437 files under
# tf-psa-crypto/ become 436, and the one that goes is
# tf-psa-crypto/drivers/builtin/src/ecp_curves_new.c. Two ADJACENT releases. With
# a glob and no manifest that upgrade just stops compiling an elliptic-curve
# source and says nothing.
#
# THREE FIELDS ON THIS PIN ANSWER THREE DIFFERENT QUESTIONS, and none of them
# substitutes for another:
#
#   sha256          did we get the BYTES we expected?
#   sources-digest  is the SET we compile the set we transcribed?
#   reviewed        has anybody asked whether those are still the bytes to WANT?
#
# The digest sees a file appear or vanish; it does NOT see a file whose contents
# changed, which is the sha256's job. A pin can be perfectly verified, compiling
# exactly the recorded set, and perfectly vulnerable.

sources-count  110
sources-digest 44997596e6cfd039f7c203be27925ad4d29e656a72b1824cb61692bd58deac5a

# --- adopting a new mbedTLS ------------------------------------------------
#
#   1. curl -sLO https://github.com/Mbed-TLS/mbedtls/releases/download/mbedtls-X.Y.Z/mbedtls-X.Y.Z.tar.bz2
#   2. sha256sum it; update `version`, `sha256`, `url`
#   3. sbcl --script scripts/mbedtls-sources.lisp <unpacked-tree>
#      -- read every GONE and NEW line against the release notes before
#         accepting them; a GONE file is one we silently stop compiling
#   4. --record the tree, update `sources-count` and `sources-digest`
#   5. bump `reviewed` -- you have just read the release notes, that IS the review
#   6. delete vendor/mbedtls/, rebuild, run the aion/tls suite on all three
#      OSes before committing
